[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc5
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Endpoint IP addressing on VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24), not the switching or routing planes. The gateway is router0 subinterface eth0.10 (172.16.10.1).

Mechanism: Both PC4 and PC5 are configured with the same IP address (172.16.10.4/24). When the gateway or peers ARP for 172.16.10.4, both hosts answer with different MACs. The ARP cache on the gateway/switch updates to whichever host replied last, so traffic for 172.16.10.4 is delivered alternately to PC4 or PC5. This produces the observed “ARP flapping” and intermittent connectivity: one host works while the other appears to drop, then it flips.

Localization and evidence:
- PC4: eth0 172.16.10.4/24, MAC 3a:9f:8e:c1:45:61; default gw 172.16.10.1. Pings to gateway succeed consistently.
- PC5: eth0 172.16.10.4/24 (should be .5), MAC 66:b7:18:49:7d:32; default gw 172.16.10.1. Command timed out during test, consistent with contention for the duplicate IP.
- Router0 ARP on eth0.10 shows 172.16.10.4 lladdr 3a:9f:8e:c1:45:61 (PC4) at the time of check, consistent with ARP pointing to one of the two hosts and subject to flipping.

Root cause: PC5 is misconfigured with a duplicate static IP (172.16.10.4), conflicting with PC4.

Corrective fix: Reconfigure PC5 to its correct unique address (172.16.10.5/24) with default gateway 172.16.10.1. After changing, bounce PC5’s interface or flush ARP caches on PC5 and router0 (ip neigh flush) to clear stale entries. Consider enabling IP/MAC guard (e.g., DHCP or port security) to prevent future duplicates. === END ===